    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d105">

                <pb facs="0171" n="95" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg95"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.00/2544</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Consul Peck to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 115</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Tsingtau</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-10-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 6, 1916</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my despatch No. 113, of
                    September 26, 1916, regarding political conditions along the Shantung Railway, I
                    have the honor to state that the rebel organization at Kaomi, 66 miles from <pb
                        facs="0172" n="96" xml:id="pg96"/> Tsingtau on the railway, submitted to the
                    Central Government some days ago, and is now engaged in disbanding troops. This
                    office has been unable to learn the details of the capitulation, but the region
                    is quiet.</p>
                <p>A copy of this despatch has been sent to the American Legation at Peking and to
                    the American Consulate at Chefoo.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Willys R. Peck</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
